% morphisms.zed -- Z rendering of the SCORE state and the Incorporation morphism.
%
% TIER A pilot (2026-08-11), first two schemas. Scope and stopping condition in the
% session plan: Z is a DRAFTING INSTRUMENT here, not a vault formality. The schemas live
% in this file; what enters the vault is (a) a `z:` frontmatter pointer and (b) prose
% rewritten under the discipline the schema imposed. That division is what keeps this
% clear of the 2026-07-24 decline recorded in hoamaint.zed ("Z as a broad vault formality
% does not enter SCORE") -- which was NOT re-read before starting. The PI's stated reason
% for proceeding without it: the question keeps returning, which is itself evidence the
% decline did not settle it. Recorded as the actual reason rather than a tidier one.
%
% Why the morphisms and not something else. OWL is a classification language (subsumption
% and disjointness; no state, no operations, no preconditions) and the Lean tier here is
% largely axiom declarations. Neither has an OPERATION semantics -- and the morphisms are
% operations. The worked case this file exists to settle: whether "sub-threshold
% incorporation" is a subclass of Incorporation (OWL's only available shape, and
% incoherent -- see obsidian/SCORE/agents/PerceptibilityThreshold.md) or a precondition
% violation. Z has a mandatory precondition slot, so the question cannot be deferred.
%
% Mirrors, name for name:
%   - vault : obsidian/SCORE/morphisms/Morphisms.md, .../agents/PerceptibilityThreshold.md
%   - OWL   : formal/score-core.owl (core:Incorporation, SC-G-16)
%   - Lean  : formal/Formal/Score/InscriptionMarket.lean (perceptibilityThreshold, canPerceive)
%
% Type-check (CZT toolchain lives in protocol-formal-template/z):
%   & ~\Dev\protocol-formal-template\z\run-czt.ps1 -File .\morphisms.zed
%
% [IDIOM A] state schema, [IDIOM B] init, [IDIOM C] guarded ops,
% [IDIOM D] characteristic subsets as horizontal schemas -- per protocol-formal-template/z.

\section{morphisms parents standard\_toolkit}

Agents, the items that can occupy a manifold or be inscribed, and the
physical marks that carry inscriptions.

\begin{zed}
  [AGENT, ITEM, MARK]
\end{zed}

The reflexive-transitive doctrinal order of B3RegionGeometry:
(x \mapsto y) \in dsub reads "x is a doctrinal substrate that y composes
from" (x \leq_d y). Already Lean-proved in Core.lean (IsRegion,
region\_substrate\_closed\_and\_graded).

\begin{axdef}
  dsub : ITEM \rel ITEM
\where
  \forall x : ITEM @ (x \mapsto x) \in dsub \\
  \forall x, y, z : ITEM @
    (x \mapsto y) \in dsub \land (y \mapsto z) \in dsub \implies (x \mapsto z) \in dsub
\end{axdef}

The reflexive-depth bar that gates B2-referential inscription
(DomainTrichotomy: inscribeB1Ref has NO depth precondition -- animals
inscribe it; only inscribeB2Ref is gated).

\begin{axdef}
  dbar : \nat
\end{axdef}

\subsection{State [IDIOM A]}

The SCORE state. Cardinality follows DomainTrichotomy \S{} Cardinality:
b1 and b3 are shared singletons (one each, internally rich); b2 is an
agent-indexed family, so it is a TOTAL function on AGENT.

delta is the per-agent perceptibility threshold (FA3); salience is the
magnitude at which an item would register FOR A GIVEN AGENT -- the pair
is agent-indexed because delta is per-agent and the filter is
manifold-determined.

\begin{schema}{Score}
  b1 : \power MARK \\
  b2 : AGENT \fun \power ITEM \\
  b3 : \power ITEM \\
  carrier : ITEM \pfun MARK \\
  delta : AGENT \fun \nat \\
  salience : AGENT \cross ITEM \fun \nat \\
  presents : MARK \pfun ITEM \\
  depth : AGENT \fun \nat \\
  b2ref : \power ITEM
\where
  \dom carrier = b3 \\
  \ran carrier \subseteq b1
\end{schema}

presents is what a B1 mark presents AS, when taken up by perception --
the B1-side analogue of an item. It is partial: not every mark presents
anything.

SCOPE, and it is stronger than "not modelled" (DEF-01/R2', 2026-09-06).
presents is NOT AGENT-INDEXED: its type is MARK -/-> ITEM, so every agent
perceiving the same mark obtains the same item. Combined with b2/b3 sharing
one ITEM sort, this means the schemas here model the MAGNITUDE gate only --
salience against delta -- and the QUALITATIVE filter is not merely left open
by this file, it is UNMODELLABLE IN THIS STATE. What the schema licenses saying is
narrower than a prescription: for two agents to obtain different content from
one mark, the content obtained must DEPEND ON THE AGENT, and nothing in this
state can. Agent-indexing presents (AGENT x MARK -/-> ITEM) is the cheapest
route, not the only one -- an agent-relative construal step over an
agent-independent presents would also serve, and which is right is open.
Either way it is a different change from splitting ITEM into B2- and
B3-content sorts: the sort split addresses the type confusion and does
nothing for agent relativity.

Two consequences worth stating so this file is not over-read.
(a) Do not cite these schemas on HOLONOMY or on STATE IDENTITY. The i != j
divergence of B2_i -> B3 -> B2_j that GaugeStructure names as its curvature
candidate has no referent here, by construction rather than by omission.
(b) The vault DOES commit to the qualitative axis in prose and anchors it
empirically (Percept.md; Homan-Maaike; Boiman-Yeshurun), and no formal layer
expresses it -- Lean's incorporate permits deformation but no axiom requires
it, and OWL has no operation semantics. The spine's position is therefore
UNDECIDED rather than settled; revisit when S1-A3 v2 is scoped, since that
amendment covers the Segment 1 spec, the engine and the Master Glossary and
does not mention Z, Lean or OWL. See core/overview/POLARIS_Overview.md
Appendix C for the ledger gap this leaves. b2ref is the referent typing (Morphisms \S{} Inscription):
items whose content is about the agent's own manifold, with no B1
referent. Its complement is the B1-referential floor.

The two invariants are the anti-Platonic commitment (category error 2)
stated as state, not as prose: every inscription HAS a carrier
(dom carrier = b3), and every carrier is a mark actually present in b1
(ran carrier \subseteq b1). B3 is therefore neither free-floating nor
identified with its carrier -- the map is total onto b3 and into b1, and
it is not required to be injective, which is multiple realizability.

\subsection{Initial state [IDIOM B]}

Nothing inscribed, no marks, empty manifolds. The invariants hold
vacuously, which is the check that they are not accidentally
unsatisfiable.

\begin{schema}{InitScore}
  Score
\where
  b3 = \{\} \\
  carrier = \{\} \\
  b1 = \{\}
\end{schema}

Stated unprimed, as an initial-state predicate on Score rather than as a
state change from nowhere -- matching InitMaint in hoamaint.zed. The
primed form (Score' with b3' etc.) is what this schema said until
2026-08-11 and it does NOT typecheck here: CZT binds primed components
introduced by \Delta, but reports a bare decorated schema inclusion as
`Undeclared name: Score'`. That was invisible while the typechecker was
not being invoked at all.

\subsection{Incorporation [IDIOM C]}

B3 -> B2. The precondition is the whole point of this file.

Three conjuncts guard it: the item must actually be inscribed (i? \in b3),
the agent must not already carry it (this is an uptake, not a no-op), and
the registered magnitude must CLEAR that agent's threshold. The third is
the one OWL cannot state.

The frame says what incorporation leaves alone: b1, b3 and carrier are
untouched. Incorporation is a B2-side operation only -- it reads the
shared substrate without modifying it.

\begin{schema}{Incorporate}
  \Delta Score \\
  a? : AGENT \\
  i? : ITEM
\where
  i? \in b3 \\
  i? \notin b2~a? \\
  salience(a? \mapsto i?) > delta~a? \\
  b2' = b2 \oplus \{ a? \mapsto (b2~a?) \cup \{i?\} \} \\
  b1' = b1 \\
  b3' = b3 \\
  carrier' = carrier
\end{schema}

NOTE -- delta' and salience' are deliberately LEFT UNCONSTRAINED, and
that is a finding rather than an omission. delta is manifold-determined
("given current manifold grain"), and this operation changes the
manifold. So an incorporation may change the incorporating agent's own
threshold, and may change what any item would subsequently register at.
The vault states neither. Writing `delta' = delta` here would assert a
no-feedback claim nobody has made; leaving it loose says only that this
schema does not determine it. See the open question raised in
Morphisms.md.

\subsection{The sub-threshold case [IDIOM D]}

The characteristic subset where a delivered item does not clear the
agent's floor.

\begin{zed}
  SubThreshold == [ Score; a? : AGENT; i? : ITEM |
                    i? \in b3 \land salience(a? \mapsto i?) \leq delta~a? ]
\end{zed}

This is the resolution the file was written for. SubThreshold and
pre Incorporate are DISJOINT by construction: the third precondition
conjunct is the negation of SubThreshold's second. So a sub-threshold
delivery is not an Incorporation that happened weakly -- it is a state in
which the Incorporation operation is NOT APPLICABLE. There is nothing to
subclass, which is why the OWL formulation had nowhere to go.

Delivery that fails its precondition still leaves b3 and carrier standing:
the inscription exists, was carried, and did not land. That is the
distinction between "no signal, not a weak signal" (Percept) and "the
mark was never made" -- and it is visible here as the difference between
a precondition violation and an unsatisfied dom carrier.

\subsection{Perception [IDIOM C]}

B1 -> B2. Structurally the twin of Incorporate: same guard, different
source. That the two schemas differ ONLY in where the item comes from
(b1 via presents, vs b3 directly) is DomainTrichotomy \S{} Cardinality's
"one mechanism on the two shared sources" -- derived here rather than
asserted.

\begin{schema}{Perceive}
  \Delta Score \\
  a? : AGENT \\
  m? : MARK
\where
  m? \in b1 \\
  m? \in \dom presents \\
  presents~m? \notin b2~a? \\
  salience(a? \mapsto presents~m?) > delta~a? \\
  b2' = b2 \oplus \{ a? \mapsto (b2~a?) \cup \{presents~m?\} \} \\
  b1' = b1 \\
  b3' = b3 \\
  carrier' = carrier \\
  presents' = presents \\
  b2ref' = b2ref
\end{schema}

\subsection{Action [IDIOM C]}

B2 -> B1. Makes a mark and establishes NO carrier -- which is exactly
what distinguishes it from Inscribe below. Both write to b1; only
inscription binds the mark to content.

\begin{schema}{Act}
  \Delta Score \\
  a? : AGENT \\
  i? : ITEM \\
  m! : MARK
\where
  i? \in b2~a? \\
  m! \notin b1 \\
  b1' = b1 \cup \{m!\} \\
  b2' = b2 \\
  b3' = b3 \\
  carrier' = carrier \\
  presents' = presents \\
  b2ref' = b2ref
\end{schema}

\subsection{Inscription [IDIOM C]}

B2 -> B3, with the referent gate. The b2ref conjunct is the
SufficientDepth condition: B1-referential inscription is ungated (the B3
floor -- animals inscribe it), B2-referential inscription requires depth.

\begin{schema}{Inscribe}
  \Delta Score \\
  a? : AGENT \\
  i? : ITEM \\
  m! : MARK
\where
  i? \in b2~a? \\
  i? \notin b3 \\
  m! \notin b1 \\
  i? \in b2ref \implies depth~a? \geq dbar \\
  b1' = b1 \cup \{m!\} \\
  b3' = b3 \cup \{i?\} \\
  carrier' = carrier \cup \{ i? \mapsto m! \} \\
  b2' = b2 \\
  presents' = presents \\
  b2ref' = b2ref
\end{schema}

FORCED, not chosen: b1' = b1 \cup \{m!\}. The state invariant
ran carrier \subseteq b1 makes it impossible to write Inscribe WITHOUT
extending b1 -- drop that conjunct and the schema is unsatisfiable. This
answers the open "Morphism implication" in B3-Inscription.md: the clean
B2 -> B3 typing and the B1 mark are not in tension, because the typing
names the DOMAINS RELATED and the frame names the STATE TOUCHED, and
those are different questions. Inscription relates B2 to B3 and touches
B1 on the way. Nothing had to be reconciled; two different things were
being called the same thing.

\subsection{Transformation [IDIOM C]}

(B2 \cross B3) -> B3. The product morphism: an existing inscription and
the agent's own holdings are both required inputs.

\begin{schema}{Transform}
  \Delta Score \\
  a? : AGENT \\
  i? : ITEM \\
  o! : ITEM \\
  m! : MARK
\where
  i? \in b3 \\
  i? \in b2~a? \\
  o! \notin b3 \\
  m! \notin b1 \\
  (i? \mapsto o!) \in dsub \\
  \forall x : ITEM @ (x \mapsto o!) \in dsub \implies x \in (b2~a?) \cup \{o!\} \\
  b1' = b1 \cup \{m!\} \\
  b3' = b3 \cup \{o!\} \\
  carrier' = carrier \cup \{ o! \mapsto m! \} \\
  b2' = b2 \\
  presents' = presents \\
  b2ref' = b2ref
\end{schema}

Three conditions carry TransformationMorphism.md's content:

  (i? \mapsto o!) \in dsub    continuity -- the output composes FROM the
                              input, which is what "recognizably derived,
                              not independently generated" means

  \forall x ... \in dsub ...  down-set preservation -- every doctrinal
                              substrate of the output is already held.
                              This is the note's own validity criterion
                              (open question 2, resolved 2026-07-18) as a
                              PRECONDITION rather than a side condition

  b3' = b3 \cup \{o!\}        the input PERSISTS. "Modifies rather than
                              replaces" is a frame statement, and this is
                              it

Two things the schema exposes. First, the down-closure is written against
b2~a? -- ONE AGENT's holdings -- whereas B3RegionGeometry states it
against a community region R(C). For a single-agent operation those are
not the same set, and the note does not say which is meant. Second,
Transform's frame is identical to Inscribe's; only its preconditions
differ. That is the formal content of "not reducible to a composition of
Incorporation and Inscription" (open question 1): the irreducibility is
entirely in the guard, not in what the operation does to the state.

\subsection{Standing note on the loose components}

delta', salience' and depth' are left UNCONSTRAINED in every operation
above, for the reason given under Incorporate: all three are
manifold-determined, and these operations change manifolds. Writing
`delta' = delta` in five schemas would assert a global no-feedback claim
that nothing in the vault supports.
